# Circular and Rotational Motion: Motion That Turns

> Motion is not always straight-line. Objects loop loops (circular
> motion) and spin about an axis (rotational motion) — from a planet
> orbiting the Sun to a ceiling fan's blades. This lesson builds the
> vocabulary, the forces, and the conservation laws for both.

## 1. Describing Rotation: Angular Quantities

A body moving in a circle is described with angles instead of
distances. Let the radius be $r$ and the angle swept be $\theta$.

| Quantity | Symbol | Unit | Straight-line analogue |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Angular displacement | $\theta$ | radian (rad) | distance $s$ |
| Angular velocity | $\omega$ | rad/s | velocity $v$ |
| Angular acceleration | $\alpha$ | rad/s² | acceleration $a$ |

[definition]
**Angular velocity** $\omega$ is the rate of change of angular
displacement: $\omega = \theta / t$. One full turn is $2\pi$ rad, so a
body completing $n$ revolutions per second has $\omega = 2\pi n$.
[/definition]

The link between linear and angular descriptions of the same motion is
the radius:

@math label="eq:v-omega-r"
v = \omega r
@/math

## 2. Uniform Circular Motion and Centripetal Force

In uniform circular motion the speed is constant but the velocity is
not — its direction changes continuously, so there is always an
acceleration **towards the centre**, the **centripetal acceleration**:

@math label="eq:centripetal-acceleration"
a_c = \frac{v^2}{r} = \omega^2 r
@/math

By Newton's second law, a real force must supply this acceleration.
That force — directed to the centre — is the **centripetal force**:

@math label="eq:centripetal-force"
F_c = \frac{mv^2}{r} = m\omega^2 r
@/math

[important]
Centripetal force is not a new kind of force. It is the *role* played
by whatever real force happens to point towards the centre: tension in
a whirled string, gravity for a satellite, friction between tyres and
road for a car on a flat curve.
[/important]

## 3. Banked Roads and the Speed of Turns

On a flat road, friction alone provides the centripetal force, which
caps how fast a car can turn. Roads and velodrome tracks are **banked**
so that a component of the normal reaction does part of the job. For a
road banked at angle $\theta$ (no friction needed):

@math label="eq:banking"
v = \sqrt{rg \tan\theta}
@/math

| Situation | Provides the centripetal force |
| --- | --- |
| Stone on a string | Tension |
| Satellite in orbit | Gravity |
| Car on a flat curve | Friction |
| Aircraft turning in flight | Horizontal component of lift |
| Electron orbiting a nucleus | Electrostatic attraction |

## 4. Vertical Circles: Where Speed Changes

A body swung in a **vertical circle** is not uniform circular motion —
gravity speeds it up at the bottom and slows it at the top. At the top
of the loop, both gravity and string tension point downward:

@math label="eq:vertical-top"
v_{min}^2 = gr
@/math

[tip]
For a bucket of water whirled in a vertical circle, this is why the
water stays in at the top: if $v \ge \sqrt{gr}$, the required
centripetal acceleration already exceeds $g$, so the water presses
into the bucket instead of falling out.
[/tip]

## 5. Rotational Motion: Torque and Moment of Inertia

For spinning bodies, Newton's laws are re-cast in angular form. The
rotational analogue of force is **torque** $\tau$, and the analogue of
mass is the **moment of inertia** $I$ — which also depends on *how far*
the mass sits from the axis.

| Concept | Straight-line form | Rotational form |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Inertia | mass $m$ | $I = \Sigma mr^2$ |
| Newton's second law | $F = ma$ | $\tau = I\alpha$ |
| Momentum | $p = mv$ | $L = I\omega$ |
| Kinetic energy | $\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ | $\frac{1}{2}I\omega^2$ |

Moments of inertia for standard shapes (about the symmetry axis):

| Body | $I$ |
| --- | --- |
| Ring | $MR^2$ |
| Disc | $\frac{1}{2}MR^2$ |
| Solid sphere | $\frac{2}{5}MR^2$ |
| Rod about its centre | $\frac{1}{12}ML^2$ |

[definition]
**Torque** (moment of force) about an axis is
$\tau = rF\sin\phi$, where $r$ is the distance from the axis to the
point where the force acts and $\phi$ is the angle between the force
and the radius. A longer handle (larger $r$) gives more torque from the
same push — that is why spanners have long handles and door knobs sit
far from hinges.
[/definition]

## 6. Angular Momentum and Its Conservation

[important]
The **angular momentum** $L = I\omega$ of a system stays constant when
no external torque acts on it: $I\omega = $ constant.
[/important]

This single rule explains a classic set of observations:

- A spinning skater pulls her arms in — $I$ drops, so $\omega$ rises:
  she spins faster.
- A diver tucked into a ball rotates quicker than when stretched out.
- The Earth's rotation slowly transfers angular momentum to the Moon
  through tides: days lengthen while the Moon recedes.

## 7. Rotational Equilibrium

A body is in rotational equilibrium when the net torque on it is zero.
A see-saw balances, not when the two weights are equal, but when their
**moments** are equal and opposite:

@math label="eq:seesaw"
m_1 r_1 = m_2 r_2
@/math

A 30 kg child sitting 2 m from the pivot balances a 60 kg adult at
1 m: $30 \times 2 = 60 \times 1$. Levers, crowbars, and weighing
balances (like the traditional *tarazu*) all work on this principle.

## 8. Satellites and Orbits

A satellite is a body in permanent free fall around a planet. Gravity
supplies exactly the centripetal force needed, which fixes the orbital
speed at radius $r$:

@math label="eq:orbital-speed"
v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}}
@/math

@chart line title="Orbital speed falls with altitude (approximate km/s)"
labels:
400 km (ISS)
2000 km
MEO (20200 km)
GEO (35786 km)

data:
7.67
6.9
3.9
3.07
@/chart

Geostationary satellites orbit at 35,786 km with $\omega$ matching
Earth's rotation, so they hover over one point on the equator — the
reason a fixed dish antenna can point at one TV satellite all day.

[summary]
Circular motion is described by angular quantities linked to linear
ones through $v = \omega r$. A net inward (centripetal) force
$mv^2/r$ is supplied by real forces like tension, gravity, or
friction. For spinning bodies, $\tau = I\alpha$ replaces $F = ma$,
rotational kinetic energy is $\frac{1}{2}I\omega^2$, and angular
momentum $I\omega$ is conserved when no external torque acts.
[/summary]
